{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-353186" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The number of cells in a petri dish $C(t)$ after $t$ hours can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}=C.\n$$\n\nIf there are currently $650$ cells in the petri dish, calculate the number of cells after $2$ hours. Round your answer to the nearest integer.\n\nThe number of cells after $2$ hours is approximately \n{{field-1}}\ncells." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037533/state.json" "file_sha256" "904167c99e60b37b1d87c6cc667dff64d383a3e708f76875a3700ce863114bad"} {"question" "q-353186" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We model the number of cells using the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}=C.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nC(t)={C}_{0}\\,{e}^{t},\n$$\n\nwhere ${C}_{0}$ is the initial value.\n\nWe're told that there are currently $650$ cells in the petri dish, so we have ${C}_{0}=650.$\n\nTherefore, the number of cells $C$ at time $t$ is\n\n$$\nC(t)=650\\,{e}^{t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the number of cells when $t=2,$ we substitute $t=2$ into the above, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C(2) & =650\\,{e}^{2} \\\\  & ≈4802.89\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRounding our answer to the nearest integer, we conclude that the number of cells after $2$ hours is approximately $4803$ cells." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037533/state.json" "file_sha256" "904167c99e60b37b1d87c6cc667dff64d383a3e708f76875a3700ce863114bad"} {"question" "q-353186" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037533/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3a511b0d4909809cdda125abdac525af68726c9502aaf7f7caa5f9a765bb2fb7"} {"question" "q-353186" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037533/state.json" "file_sha256" "904167c99e60b37b1d87c6cc667dff64d383a3e708f76875a3700ce863114bad"} {"question" "q-353186" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4803"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037533/state.json" "file_sha256" "904167c99e60b37b1d87c6cc667dff64d383a3e708f76875a3700ce863114bad"} {"question" "q-83144" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The number of subscribers $S(t)$ to a video channel after $t$ weeks can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=0.05S.\n$$\n\nIf the channel currently has $15\\,000$ subscribers, calculate the number of subscribers after $12$ weeks. Round your answer to the nearest integer." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037533/state.json" "file_sha256" "904167c99e60b37b1d87c6cc667dff64d383a3e708f76875a3700ce863114bad"} {"question" "q-83144" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We model the number of subscribers using the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=0.05S.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nS(t)={S}_{0}\\,{e}^{0.05t},\n$$\n\nwhere ${S}_{0}$ is the initial value.\n\nWe're told that the channel currently has $15\\,000$ subscribers, so we have ${S}_{0}=15\\,000.$\n\nTherefore, the number of subscribers $S$ at time $t$ is\n\n$$\nS(t)=15\\,000\\,{e}^{0.05t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the number of subscribers when $t=12,$ we substitute $t=12$ into the above, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(12) & =15\\,000\\,{e}^{0.05\\cdot 12} \\\\  & =15\\,000\\,{e}^{0.6} \\\\  & ≈27\\,331.78\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRounding our answer to the nearest integer, we conclude that the number of subscribers is $27\\,332$ after $12$ weeks." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037533/state.json" "file_sha256" "904167c99e60b37b1d87c6cc667dff64d383a3e708f76875a3700ce863114bad"} {"question" "q-83144" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037533/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3a511b0d4909809cdda125abdac525af68726c9502aaf7f7caa5f9a765bb2fb7"} {"question" "q-83144" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037533/state.json" "file_sha256" "904167c99e60b37b1d87c6cc667dff64d383a3e708f76875a3700ce863114bad"} {"question" "q-83144" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "20\\,248"] ["math" "22\\,377"] ["math" "24\\,731"] ["math" "27\\,332"] ["math" "31\\,755"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037533/state.json" "file_sha256" "904167c99e60b37b1d87c6cc667dff64d383a3e708f76875a3700ce863114bad"} {"question" "q-83144" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "27\\,332"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037533/state.json" "file_sha256" "904167c99e60b37b1d87c6cc667dff64d383a3e708f76875a3700ce863114bad"} {"question" "q-119231" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The number of subscribers $S(t)$ to a certain site's e-mail news service after $t$ weeks can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=0.01S.\n$$\n\nIf the site's e-mail news service currently has $10\\,000$ subscribers, calculate the number of subscribers after $10$ weeks. Round your answer to the nearest integer." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037533/state.json" "file_sha256" "904167c99e60b37b1d87c6cc667dff64d383a3e708f76875a3700ce863114bad"} {"question" "q-119231" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We model the number of subscribers using the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=0.01S.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nS(t)={S}_{0}\\,{e}^{0.01t},\n$$\n\nwhere ${S}_{0}$ is the initial value.\n\nWe're told that the service currently has $10\\,000$ subscribers, so we have ${S}_{0}=10\\,000.$\n\nTherefore, the number of subscribers $S$ at time $t$ is\n\n$$\nS(t)=10\\,000\\,{e}^{0.01t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the number of subscribers when $t=10,$ we substitute $t=10$ into the above, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(10) & =10\\,000\\,{e}^{0.01\\cdot 10} \\\\  & =10\\,000\\,{e}^{0.1} \\\\  & ≈11\\,051.71\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRounding our answer to the nearest integer, we conclude that the number of subscribers is $11\\,052$ after $10$ weeks." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037533/state.json" "file_sha256" "904167c99e60b37b1d87c6cc667dff64d383a3e708f76875a3700ce863114bad"} {"question" "q-119231" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037533/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3a511b0d4909809cdda125abdac525af68726c9502aaf7f7caa5f9a765bb2fb7"} {"question" "q-119231" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037533/state.json" "file_sha256" "904167c99e60b37b1d87c6cc667dff64d383a3e708f76875a3700ce863114bad"} {"question" "q-119231" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "10\\,981"] ["math" "11\\,052"] ["math" "12\\,231"] ["math" "13\\,002"] ["math" "14\\,564"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037533/state.json" "file_sha256" "904167c99e60b37b1d87c6cc667dff64d383a3e708f76875a3700ce863114bad"} {"question" "q-119231" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "11\\,052"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037533/state.json" "file_sha256" "904167c99e60b37b1d87c6cc667dff64d383a3e708f76875a3700ce863114bad"} {"question" "q-353180" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The amount of a rare plant species $A(t)$ after $t$ years can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=0.072A.\n$$\n\nIf there are currently $320$ plants, calculate the amount after $9$ years. Round your answer to the nearest integer.\n\nThe amount of the plant species after $9$ years is approximately \n{{field-1}}\nplants." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037533/state.json" "file_sha256" "904167c99e60b37b1d87c6cc667dff64d383a3e708f76875a3700ce863114bad"} {"question" "q-353180" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We model the amount using the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=0.072A.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nA(t)={A}_{0}\\,{e}^{0.072t},\n$$\n\nwhere ${A}_{0}$ is the initial value.\n\nWe're told that there are currently $320$ plants, so we have ${A}_{0}=320.$\n\nTherefore, the amount $A$ at time $t$ is\n\n$$\nA(t)=320\\,{e}^{0.072t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the amount when $t=9,$ we substitute $t=9$ into the above, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(9) & =320\\,{e}^{0.072\\cdot 9} \\\\  & =320\\,{e}^{0.648} \\\\  & ≈611.53\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRounding our answer to the nearest integer, we conclude that the amount of the plant species after $9$ years is approximately $612$ plants." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037533/state.json" "file_sha256" "904167c99e60b37b1d87c6cc667dff64d383a3e708f76875a3700ce863114bad"} {"question" "q-353180" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037533/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3a511b0d4909809cdda125abdac525af68726c9502aaf7f7caa5f9a765bb2fb7"} {"question" "q-353180" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037533/state.json" "file_sha256" "904167c99e60b37b1d87c6cc667dff64d383a3e708f76875a3700ce863114bad"} {"question" "q-353180" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "612"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037533/state.json" "file_sha256" "904167c99e60b37b1d87c6cc667dff64d383a3e708f76875a3700ce863114bad"} {"question" "q-353189" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The number of insects in a controlled habitat $H(t)$ after $t$ months can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}=2H.\n$$\n\nIf there are currently $120$ insects in the habitat, calculate the number of insects after $2$ months. Round your answer to the nearest integer.\n\nThe number of insects after $2$ months is approximately \n{{field-1}}\ninsects." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037533/state.json" "file_sha256" "904167c99e60b37b1d87c6cc667dff64d383a3e708f76875a3700ce863114bad"} {"question" "q-353189" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We model the number of insects using the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}=2H.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nH(t)={H}_{0}\\,{e}^{2t},\n$$\n\nwhere ${H}_{0}$ is the initial value.\n\nWe're told that there are currently $120$ insects in the habitat, so we have ${H}_{0}=120.$\n\nTherefore, the number of insects $H$ at time $t$ is\n\n$$\nH(t)=120\\,{e}^{2t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the number of insects when $t=2,$ we substitute $t=2$ into the above, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}H(2) & =120\\,{e}^{2\\cdot 2} \\\\  & =120\\,{e}^{4} \\\\  & ≈6551.78\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRounding our answer to the nearest integer, we conclude that the number of insects after $2$ months is approximately $6552$ insects." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037533/state.json" "file_sha256" "904167c99e60b37b1d87c6cc667dff64d383a3e708f76875a3700ce863114bad"} {"question" "q-353189" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037533/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3a511b0d4909809cdda125abdac525af68726c9502aaf7f7caa5f9a765bb2fb7"} {"question" "q-353189" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037533/state.json" "file_sha256" "904167c99e60b37b1d87c6cc667dff64d383a3e708f76875a3700ce863114bad"} {"question" "q-353189" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "6552"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037533/state.json" "file_sha256" "904167c99e60b37b1d87c6cc667dff64d383a3e708f76875a3700ce863114bad"} {"question" "q-119341" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The number of sales $S(t)$ of a new tech device $t$ weeks after the start of sales can be modeled by the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=-0.1S.\n$$\n\nIf there were $100\\,000$ sales initially, calculate the number of sales after $10$ weeks. Round your answer to the nearest integer." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037533/state.json" "file_sha256" "904167c99e60b37b1d87c6cc667dff64d383a3e708f76875a3700ce863114bad"} {"question" "q-119341" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We model the number of sales using the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=-0.1S.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nS(t)={S}_{0}\\,{e}^{-0.1t},\n$$\n\nwhere ${S}_{0}$ is the initial value.\n\nWe're told that there were $100\\,000$  sales initially, so we have ${S}_{0}=100\\,000.$\n\nTherefore, the number of sales $S$ at time $t$ is\n\n$$\nS(t)=100\\,000\\,{e}^{-0.1t}\\,.\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the number of sales when $t=10,$ we substitute $t=10$ into the above, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(10) & =100\\,000\\,{e}^{-0.1\\cdot 10} \\\\  & =100\\,000\\,{e}^{-1} \\\\  & ≈36\\,787.94\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRounding our answer to the nearest integer, we conclude that there are $36\\,788$ sales after $10$ weeks." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037533/state.json" "file_sha256" "904167c99e60b37b1d87c6cc667dff64d383a3e708f76875a3700ce863114bad"} {"question" "q-119341" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037533/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3a511b0d4909809cdda125abdac525af68726c9502aaf7f7caa5f9a765bb2fb7"} {"question" "q-119341" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037533/state.json" "file_sha256" "904167c99e60b37b1d87c6cc667dff64d383a3e708f76875a3700ce863114bad"} {"question" "q-119341" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "36\\,788"] ["math" "42\\,125"] ["math" "52\\,009"] ["math" "67\\,124"] ["math" "73\\,558"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037533/state.json" "file_sha256" "904167c99e60b37b1d87c6cc667dff64d383a3e708f76875a3700ce863114bad"} {"question" "q-119341" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "36\\,788"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037533/state.json" "file_sha256" "904167c99e60b37b1d87c6cc667dff64d383a3e708f76875a3700ce863114bad"} {"question" "q-353197" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The population of a bacterial culture $P(t)$ after $t$ hours can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=-P.\n$$\n\nIf there are currently $30$ bacteria in the culture, calculate the population after $3$ hours. Round your answer to the nearest integer.\n\nThe population after $3$ hours is approximately \n{{field-1}}\nbacteria." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037533/state.json" "file_sha256" "904167c99e60b37b1d87c6cc667dff64d383a3e708f76875a3700ce863114bad"} {"question" "q-353197" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We model the population using the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=-P.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)={P}_{0}\\,{e}^{-t},\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{0}$ is the initial value.\n\nWe're told that there are currently $30$ bacteria in the culture, so we have ${P}_{0}=30.$\n\nTherefore, the population $P$ at time $t$ is\n\n$$\nP(t)=30\\,{e}^{-t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the population when $t=3,$ we substitute $t=3$ into the above, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(3) & =30\\,{e}^{-1\\cdot 3} \\\\  & =30\\,{e}^{-3} \\\\  & ≈1.49\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRounding our answer to the nearest integer, we conclude that the population after $3$ hours is approximately $1$ bacterium." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037533/state.json" "file_sha256" "904167c99e60b37b1d87c6cc667dff64d383a3e708f76875a3700ce863114bad"} {"question" "q-353197" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037533/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3a511b0d4909809cdda125abdac525af68726c9502aaf7f7caa5f9a765bb2fb7"} {"question" "q-353197" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037533/state.json" "file_sha256" "904167c99e60b37b1d87c6cc667dff64d383a3e708f76875a3700ce863114bad"} {"question" "q-353197" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037533/state.json" "file_sha256" "904167c99e60b37b1d87c6cc667dff64d383a3e708f76875a3700ce863114bad"} {"question" "q-83140" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The outstanding amount $L(t)$ that Daphne needs to repay the bank $t$ years after taking out a loan can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}t}=-0.5L.\n$$\n\nIf she borrowed $$32\\,000,$ calculate the debt remaining after $8$ years. Round your answer to the nearest dollar." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037533/state.json" "file_sha256" "904167c99e60b37b1d87c6cc667dff64d383a3e708f76875a3700ce863114bad"} {"question" "q-83140" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We model the amount of debt remaining using the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}t}=-0.5L.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nL(t)={L}_{0}\\,{e}^{-0.5t},\n$$\n\nwhere ${L}_{0}$ is the initial value.\n\nWe're told that $$32\\,000$ were borrowed, so we have ${L}_{0}=32\\,000.$\n\nTherefore, the amount of debt $L$ remaining at time $t$ is\n\n$$\nL(t)=32\\,000\\,{e}^{-0.5t}\\,.\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the amount of debt remaining when $t=8,$ we substitute $t=8$ into the above, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(8) & =32\\,000\\,{e}^{-0.5\\cdot 8} \\\\  & =32\\,000\\,{e}^{-4} \\\\  & ≈586.100\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRounding our answer to the nearest integer, we conclude that $586$ dollars of debt remain." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037533/state.json" "file_sha256" "904167c99e60b37b1d87c6cc667dff64d383a3e708f76875a3700ce863114bad"} {"question" "q-83140" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037533/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3a511b0d4909809cdda125abdac525af68726c9502aaf7f7caa5f9a765bb2fb7"} {"question" "q-83140" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037533/state.json" "file_sha256" "904167c99e60b37b1d87c6cc667dff64d383a3e708f76875a3700ce863114bad"} {"question" "q-83140" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$$125$"] ["text" "$$216$"] ["text" "$$355$"] ["text" "$$586$"] ["text" "$$966$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037533/state.json" "file_sha256" "904167c99e60b37b1d87c6cc667dff64d383a3e708f76875a3700ce863114bad"} {"question" "q-83140" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$$586$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037533/state.json" "file_sha256" "904167c99e60b37b1d87c6cc667dff64d383a3e708f76875a3700ce863114bad"} {"question" "q-15208" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The number of particles $N(t)$ in a radioactive substance after $t$ years can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=-3N.\n$$\n\nIf there were $8\\,500$ particles initially, calculate the number of particles remaining after $2$ years. Round your answer to the nearest integer." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037533/state.json" "file_sha256" "904167c99e60b37b1d87c6cc667dff64d383a3e708f76875a3700ce863114bad"} {"question" "q-15208" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We model the number of particles in the substance using the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=-3N.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nN(t)={N}_{0}\\,{e}^{-3t},\n$$\n\nwhere ${N}_{0}$ is the initial value.\n\nWe're told that there were $8\\,500$ particles initially, so we have ${N}_{0}=8\\,500.$\n\nTherefore, the number of particles $N$ at time $t$ is\n\n$$\nN(t)=8\\,500\\,{e}^{-3t}\\,.\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the number of particles when $t=2,$ we substitute $t=2$ into the above, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(2) & =8\\,500\\,{e}^{-3\\cdot 2} \\\\  & =8\\,500\\,{e}^{-6} \\\\  & ≈21.069\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRounding our answer to the nearest integer, we conclude that $21$ particles remain." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037533/state.json" "file_sha256" "904167c99e60b37b1d87c6cc667dff64d383a3e708f76875a3700ce863114bad"} {"question" "q-15208" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037533/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3a511b0d4909809cdda125abdac525af68726c9502aaf7f7caa5f9a765bb2fb7"} {"question" "q-15208" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037533/state.json" "file_sha256" "904167c99e60b37b1d87c6cc667dff64d383a3e708f76875a3700ce863114bad"} {"question" "q-15208" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "12"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "206"] ["math" "21"] ["math" "403"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037533/state.json" "file_sha256" "904167c99e60b37b1d87c6cc667dff64d383a3e708f76875a3700ce863114bad"} {"question" "q-15208" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "21"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037533/state.json" "file_sha256" "904167c99e60b37b1d87c6cc667dff64d383a3e708f76875a3700ce863114bad"} {"question" "q-339591" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The mass of a radioactive sample $R(t)$ (in milligrams) after $t$ days can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}R}{\\text{d}t}=-0.05R.\n$$\n\nIf the sample initially had a mass of $80$ milligrams, calculate its mass after $30$ days. Round your answer to the nearest integer." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037533/state.json" "file_sha256" "904167c99e60b37b1d87c6cc667dff64d383a3e708f76875a3700ce863114bad"} {"question" "q-339591" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We model the mass of the sample using the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}R}{\\text{d}t}=-0.05R.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nR(t)={R}_{0}\\,{e}^{-0.05t},\n$$\n\nwhere ${R}_{0}$ is the initial mass.\n\nWe're told that the initial mass of the sample is $80$ milligrams, so we have ${R}_{0}=80.$\n\nTherefore, the mass of the sample $R$ at time $t$ is\n\n$$\nR(t)=80\\,{e}^{-0.05t}\\,.\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the mass of the sample when $t=30,$ we substitute $t=30$ into the above, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(30) & =80\\,{e}^{-0.05\\cdot 30} \\\\  & =80\\,{e}^{-1.5} \\\\  & =17.850…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRounding our answer to the nearest integer, we conclude that the mass of the sample after $30$ days is $18$ milligrams." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037533/state.json" "file_sha256" "904167c99e60b37b1d87c6cc667dff64d383a3e708f76875a3700ce863114bad"} {"question" "q-339591" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037533/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3a511b0d4909809cdda125abdac525af68726c9502aaf7f7caa5f9a765bb2fb7"} {"question" "q-339591" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037533/state.json" "file_sha256" "904167c99e60b37b1d87c6cc667dff64d383a3e708f76875a3700ce863114bad"} {"question" "q-339591" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "13"] ["math" "18"] ["math" "24"] ["math" "55"] ["math" "60"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037533/state.json" "file_sha256" "904167c99e60b37b1d87c6cc667dff64d383a3e708f76875a3700ce863114bad"} {"question" "q-339591" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "18"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037533/state.json" "file_sha256" "904167c99e60b37b1d87c6cc667dff64d383a3e708f76875a3700ce863114bad"} {"question" "q-339651" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The value of a savings account $A=A(t)$ after $t$ years can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=kA,\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is the growth rate. If the account initially had $$12\\,000,$ and $A(4)=$15\\,000,$ calculate the growth rate $k$ to the nearest percent." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037533/state.json" "file_sha256" "904167c99e60b37b1d87c6cc667dff64d383a3e708f76875a3700ce863114bad"} {"question" "q-339651" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We model the value of the savings account using the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=kA.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nA(t)={A}_{0}\\,{e}^{kt},\n$$\n\nwhere ${A}_{0}$ is the initial value.\n\nWe're told that the account initially had $$12\\,000,$ so we have ${A}_{0}=12\\,000.$\n\nTherefore, the value $A$ at time $t$ is\n\n$$\nA(t)=12\\,000\\,{e}^{kt}.\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the value of $k,$ we substitute $A(4)=15\\,000$ into the equation above and solve for $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(4) & =12\\,000\\,{e}^{k\\cdot 4} \\\\ 15\\,000 & =12\\,000\\,{e}^{4k} \\\\ {e}^{4k} & =\\frac{15\\,000}{12\\,000} \\\\ {e}^{4k} & =1.25 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{4k}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1.25) \\\\ 4k & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1.25) \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1.25) \\\\ k & =0.055\\,78…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k≈6%,$ rounded to the nearest percent." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037533/state.json" "file_sha256" "904167c99e60b37b1d87c6cc667dff64d383a3e708f76875a3700ce863114bad"} {"question" "q-339651" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037533/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3a511b0d4909809cdda125abdac525af68726c9502aaf7f7caa5f9a765bb2fb7"} {"question" "q-339651" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037533/state.json" "file_sha256" "904167c99e60b37b1d87c6cc667dff64d383a3e708f76875a3700ce863114bad"} {"question" "q-339651" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "10%"] ["math" "12%"] ["math" "4%"] ["math" "6%"] ["math" "8%"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037533/state.json" "file_sha256" "904167c99e60b37b1d87c6cc667dff64d383a3e708f76875a3700ce863114bad"} {"question" "q-339651" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "6%"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037533/state.json" "file_sha256" "904167c99e60b37b1d87c6cc667dff64d383a3e708f76875a3700ce863114bad"} {"question" "q-339650" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The population of a bacterial culture $N=N(t)$ after $t$ hours can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=kN,\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is the growth rate. If the culture initially contained $500$ bacteria, and $N(3)=2\\,000,$ calculate the growth rate $k$ to the nearest percent." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037533/state.json" "file_sha256" "904167c99e60b37b1d87c6cc667dff64d383a3e708f76875a3700ce863114bad"} {"question" "q-339650" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We model the population of the bacterial culture using the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=kN.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nN(t)={N}_{0}\\,{e}^{kt},\n$$\n\nwhere ${N}_{0}$ is the initial population.\n\nWe're told that the culture initially contained $500$ bacteria, so we have ${N}_{0}=500.$\n\nTherefore, the population $N$ at time $t$ is\n\n$$\nN(t)=500\\,{e}^{kt}.\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the value of $k,$ we substitute $N(3)=2\\,000$ into the equation above and solve for $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(3) & =500\\,{e}^{k\\cdot 3} \\\\ 2\\,000 & =500\\,{e}^{3k} \\\\ {e}^{3k} & =\\frac{2\\,000}{500} \\\\ {e}^{3k} & =4 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{3k}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4) \\\\ 3k & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4) \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4) \\\\ k & =0.462\\,09…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k≈46%,$ rounded to the nearest percent." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037533/state.json" "file_sha256" "904167c99e60b37b1d87c6cc667dff64d383a3e708f76875a3700ce863114bad"} {"question" "q-339650" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037533/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3a511b0d4909809cdda125abdac525af68726c9502aaf7f7caa5f9a765bb2fb7"} {"question" "q-339650" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037533/state.json" "file_sha256" "904167c99e60b37b1d87c6cc667dff64d383a3e708f76875a3700ce863114bad"} {"question" "q-339650" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "36%"] ["math" "40%"] ["math" "46%"] ["math" "48%"] ["math" "52%"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037533/state.json" "file_sha256" "904167c99e60b37b1d87c6cc667dff64d383a3e708f76875a3700ce863114bad"} {"question" "q-339650" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "46%"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037533/state.json" "file_sha256" "904167c99e60b37b1d87c6cc667dff64d383a3e708f76875a3700ce863114bad"} {"question" "q-119597" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The value $V=V(t)$ of a certain art object $t$ years after its production can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=kV\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is the growth rate. If it was initially worth $$2\\,000,$ and $V(10)=10\\,000$, calculate the value  of the growth rate $k$ to the nearest percent." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037533/state.json" "file_sha256" "904167c99e60b37b1d87c6cc667dff64d383a3e708f76875a3700ce863114bad"} {"question" "q-119597" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We model the value of the art object using the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=kV.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nV(t)={V}_{0}\\,{e}^{kt},\n$$\n\nwhere ${V}_{0}$ is the initial value.\n\nWe're told that  it was initially worth $$2\\,000,$ so we have ${V}_{0}=2\\,000.$\n\nTherefore, the value $V$ at time $t$ is\n\n$$\nV(t)=2\\,000\\,{e}^{kt}.\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the value of $k,$ we substitute $V(10)=10\\,000$ into the equation above and solve for $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V(10) & =2\\,000\\,{e}^{k\\cdot 10} \\\\ 10\\,000 & =2\\,000\\,{e}^{10k} \\\\ {e}^{10k} & =\\frac{10\\,000}{2\\,000} \\\\ {e}^{10k} & =5 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{10k}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5) \\\\ 10k & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5) \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5) \\\\ k & ≈0.16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k≈16%,$ rounded to the nearest percent." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037533/state.json" "file_sha256" "904167c99e60b37b1d87c6cc667dff64d383a3e708f76875a3700ce863114bad"} {"question" "q-119597" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037533/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3a511b0d4909809cdda125abdac525af68726c9502aaf7f7caa5f9a765bb2fb7"} {"question" "q-119597" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037533/state.json" "file_sha256" "904167c99e60b37b1d87c6cc667dff64d383a3e708f76875a3700ce863114bad"} {"question" "q-119597" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "11%"] ["math" "16%"] ["math" "19%"] ["math" "24%"] ["math" "30%"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037533/state.json" "file_sha256" "904167c99e60b37b1d87c6cc667dff64d383a3e708f76875a3700ce863114bad"} {"question" "q-119597" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "16%"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037533/state.json" "file_sha256" "904167c99e60b37b1d87c6cc667dff64d383a3e708f76875a3700ce863114bad"} {"question" "q-353211" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The number of subscribers $S=S(t)$ to a website $t$ months after a marketing campaign can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=kS\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is the growth rate. If there were $12\\,000$ subscribers initially, and $S(2)=15\\,000$, calculate the value of the growth rate $k$ to the nearest percent.\n\n$k≈$\n{{field-1}}\n$%$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037533/state.json" "file_sha256" "904167c99e60b37b1d87c6cc667dff64d383a3e708f76875a3700ce863114bad"} {"question" "q-353211" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We model the number of subscribers using the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=kS.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nS(t)={S}_{0}\\,{e}^{kt},\n$$\n\nwhere ${S}_{0}$ is the initial value.\n\nWe're told that there were $12\\,000$ subscribers initially, so we have ${S}_{0}=12\\,000.$\n\nTherefore, the number of subscribers $S$ at time $t$ is\n\n$$\nS(t)=12\\,000\\,{e}^{kt}.\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the value of $k,$ we substitute $S(2)=15\\,000$ into the equation above and solve for $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(2) & =12\\,000\\,{e}^{k\\cdot 2} \\\\ 15\\,000 & =12\\,000\\,{e}^{2k} \\\\ {e}^{2k} & =\\frac{15\\,000}{12\\,000} \\\\ {e}^{2k} & =\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{2k}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5}{4}) \\\\ 2k & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5}{4}) \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5}{4}) \\\\ k & ≈0.111\\,6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k≈11%,$ rounded to the nearest percent." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037533/state.json" "file_sha256" "904167c99e60b37b1d87c6cc667dff64d383a3e708f76875a3700ce863114bad"} {"question" "q-353211" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037533/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3a511b0d4909809cdda125abdac525af68726c9502aaf7f7caa5f9a765bb2fb7"} {"question" "q-353211" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037533/state.json" "file_sha256" "904167c99e60b37b1d87c6cc667dff64d383a3e708f76875a3700ce863114bad"} {"question" "q-353211" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "11"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037533/state.json" "file_sha256" "904167c99e60b37b1d87c6cc667dff64d383a3e708f76875a3700ce863114bad"} {"question" "q-353210" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The number of bacteria $N=N(t)$ in a laboratory culture $t$ hours after observation can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=kN\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is the growth rate. If there were $500$ bacteria initially, and $N(3)=900$, calculate the value of the growth rate $k$ to the nearest percent.\n\n$k≈$\n{{field-1}}\n$%$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037533/state.json" "file_sha256" "904167c99e60b37b1d87c6cc667dff64d383a3e708f76875a3700ce863114bad"} {"question" "q-353210" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We model the number of bacteria using the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=kN.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nN(t)={N}_{0}\\,{e}^{kt},\n$$\n\nwhere ${N}_{0}$ is the initial value.\n\nWe're told that there were $500$ bacteria initially, so we have ${N}_{0}=500.$\n\nTherefore, the number of bacteria $N$ at time $t$ is\n\n$$\nN(t)=500\\,{e}^{kt}.\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the value of $k,$ we substitute $N(3)=900$ into the equation above and solve for $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(3) & =500\\,{e}^{k\\cdot 3} \\\\ 900 & =500\\,{e}^{3k} \\\\ {e}^{3k} & =\\frac{900}{500} \\\\ {e}^{3k} & =\\frac{9}{5} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{3k}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{9}{5}) \\\\ 3k & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{9}{5}) \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{9}{5}) \\\\ k & ≈0.1959\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k≈20%,$ rounded to the nearest percent." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037533/state.json" "file_sha256" "904167c99e60b37b1d87c6cc667dff64d383a3e708f76875a3700ce863114bad"} {"question" "q-353210" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037533/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3a511b0d4909809cdda125abdac525af68726c9502aaf7f7caa5f9a765bb2fb7"} {"question" "q-353210" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037533/state.json" "file_sha256" "904167c99e60b37b1d87c6cc667dff64d383a3e708f76875a3700ce863114bad"} {"question" "q-353210" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "20"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037533/state.json" "file_sha256" "904167c99e60b37b1d87c6cc667dff64d383a3e708f76875a3700ce863114bad"} {"question" "e-542" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose that the population $P(t)$ of a town after $t$ years can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}P} {\\textrm{d}t} = 0.02P.\n$$\n\nIf there are currently $500$ people in the town, what will be the town's population in $3$ years?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037533/example-542.json" "file_sha256" "f3505413b38547055d12be11d0b72912c30f562756958dbe98a89c52e0de55de"} {"question" "e-542" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We note that this is an exponential growth differential equation with $r = 0.02$. Therefore, the solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nP(t) = P_0\\,e^{0.02t},\n$$\n\nwhere $P_0$ is the initial value.\n\nWe're told that the population is currently $500,$ so we have $P_0 = 500.$ \n\nTherefore, the population of the town at time $t$ is\n\n$$\nP(t) = 500\\,e^{0.02t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the population after $3$ years, we substitute $t=3$ into the above, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(3) & =500\\,{e}^{0.02\\cdot 3} \\\\  & =500\\,{e}^{0.06} \\\\  & ≈531\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the population will be approximately $531$ after $3$ years." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037533/example-542.json" "file_sha256" "f3505413b38547055d12be11d0b72912c30f562756958dbe98a89c52e0de55de"} {"question" "e-7312" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose that the value $V(t)$ of a particular car can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d V}{\\textrm d t} = -0.1V,\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in years, since the car was first purchased. If the car was purchased for $\\[math]10\\,000,$ what will it be worth after $3$ years?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037533/example-7312.json" "file_sha256" "8e1d9a5d68f9ef6ab12efb437bb6a5d121eafb8e54b9d9f4019c1a949c4eacb3"} {"question" "e-7312" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that the right-hand side of the equation is *negative*, which means that the car's value is *decreasing.*\n\nThis is an exponential decay differential equation with $r = 0.1.$ Therefore, the general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nV(t) = V_0 e^{-0.1 t}.\n$$\n\nWe know that the car was first purchased for $\\[math]10\\,000.$ Therefore, $V_0 = 10\\,000,$ and we have the particular solution\n\n$$\nV(t) = 10\\,000 e^{-0.1 t}.\n$$\n\nTo find the car's value after $3$ years, we substitute $t=3$ into the particular solution above. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V(3) & =10\\,000{e}^{-0.1\\cdot 3} \\\\  & =10\\,000{e}^{-0.3} \\\\  & ≈7\\,408.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the car will be worth approximately $\\[/math]7\\,408$ after three years." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037533/example-7312.json" "file_sha256" "8e1d9a5d68f9ef6ab12efb437bb6a5d121eafb8e54b9d9f4019c1a949c4eacb3"} {"question" "e-7273" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The number of followers of a celebrity's social media account increases at a rate that is directly proportional to the number of followers at that time. Given that there are currently $600\\,000$ followers and in $3$ months there will be $780\\,000$ followers, what is the growth rate? Round your answer to the nearest percent." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037533/example-7273.json" "file_sha256" "50defff01d029dba92bfa446b8b9b7988a8a7000f187b273cc035644c5be6842"} {"question" "e-7273" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The rate $N'(t)$ at which the number of followers increases is directly proportional to the number of followers $N.$ Therefore, we can model the number of followers using the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d} N}{\\mathrm{d} t} = rN,\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is measured in months, and $r > 0$ is the growth rate.\n\nThe solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nN(t) = N_0\\,e^{rt},\n$$\n\nwhere $N_0$ is the initial number of followers.\n\nWe're told that there are currently $600\\,000$ followers, so we have $N_0 = 600\\,000.$\n\nTherefore, the number of followers $N$ after $t$ months is\n\n$$\nN(t) = 600\\,000 \\, e^{rt} .\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the growth rate, we substitute $N(3)=780\\,000$ into the equation above and solve for $r,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(3) & =600\\,000\\,{e}^{r\\cdot 3} \\\\ 780\\,000 & =600\\,000\\,{e}^{3r} \\\\ {e}^{3r} & =\\frac{780\\,000}{600\\,000} \\\\ {e}^{3r} & =1.3 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{3r}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1.3) \\\\ 3r & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1.3) \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1.3) \\\\ r & ≈0.087\\,54\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $r\\approx 9\\%,$ rounded to the nearest percent." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037533/example-7273.json" "file_sha256" "50defff01d029dba92bfa446b8b9b7988a8a7000f187b273cc035644c5be6842"} {"question" "q-83140" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$$586$"] "category" "reviewed_ma_solution" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037533/answer-source-reviews.json" "file_sha256" "d2cd2a9f56082588f50068a993504e3efa3fd593ed706752dab014a980d9a32c" "reviewer_session" "01a118ea-3923-70c2-acd9-0830602bde5f" "rationale" "Solving L'=-0.5L with L(0)=32000 gives L(t)=32000e^(-0.5t). Thus L(8)=32000e^(-4)=586.100444..., which rounds to 586 dollars. Original option b (zero-based index 1) displays $586. The authentic worked solution and successful grading confirm this choice. Preserve its exact captured text value \"$$586$\" and its text field type." "bindings" {"problem" "5f270588742def28ffe99a71f18201b203e9bf2ca865f6317cfd5229c4423306" "worked_solution" "a3141e8dd1fbedbe9bf752148167009a28c2f6e2b6ad6f0079d6730aee75430a" "original_field" "a7683d1cadec659fc53af2ad1095c879ec48f37bd83a39a35b46469b3bcf3b31" "reviewed_field" "6f484f39655eea1f205332cd4180f01d3b2a6d76a421f9570c8607c72119c54f" "raw_widget" "44f9771544a66b75bcef21732f3c0ac3512027876087840d7bdc8c4453c9b048"} "evidence_files" [{"path" "q-83140-before.png" "sha256" "c491983f278728c1c65f36f9351640a60c27401b9072eab4c36425de21b2da60"} {"path" "q-83140-before.json" "sha256" "d26be13dd2e9195b4830da329a333f0c7cf80da42b5f7419a2d365ac4b31345b"} {"path" "q-83140-after.png" "sha256" "47966821de3d0d65bf08fddd6db8524cd1750cffd75bc7eb25d7bba377bee4f4"} {"path" "history-q-83140.png" "sha256" "38fb63647e17e163440543b7fed31061cc3ea7a89a450eb0a7aba3bdc66fc17d"} {"path" "edb-import/questions.edn" "sha256" "141634610c4c198a07127314746516c9cf973bdc95f62eae886a0983cbfed1ab"}]} {"question" "q-83140" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$$125$"] ["text" "$$216$"] ["text" "$$355$"] ["text" "$$586$"] ["text" "$$966$"]] "category" "reviewed_ma_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037533/answer-source-reviews.json" "file_sha256" "d2cd2a9f56082588f50068a993504e3efa3fd593ed706752dab014a980d9a32c" "rationale" "Solving L'=-0.5L with L(0)=32000 gives L(t)=32000e^(-0.5t). Thus L(8)=32000e^(-4)=586.100444..., which rounds to 586 dollars. Original option b (zero-based index 1) displays $586. The authentic worked solution and successful grading confirm this choice. Preserve its exact captured text value \"$$586$\" and its text field type."}] "usage" {"e-542" [] "q-83140" [] "e-7273" [] "q-15208" [] "e-7312" []} "basis" 1272 "retractions" [[17592186088358 :question/problem "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nThe outstanding amount $L(t)$ that Daphne needs to repay the bank $t$ years after taking out a loan can be modeled by the differential equation\n$\\frac{dL}{dt} =-0.5L$.\n\nIf she borrowed $32000$ dollars, calculate the debt remaining after $8$ years. Round your answer to the nearest dollar."] [17592186088358 :question/worked-solution "The solution of $L^{\\prime}=-0.5L$ is $L(t)=Ce^{-0.5t}$. The initial debt gives $C=32000$, so $L(8)=32000e^{-4}\\approx586.100$. The remaining debt is $586$ dollars to the nearest dollar."] [17592186088358 :question/answer-fields 17592186087078] [17592186087955 :question/problem "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nThe number of particles $N(t)$ in a radioactive substance after $t$ years can be modeled by the differential equation\n$\\frac{dN}{dt} =-3N$.\nIf there were $8500$ particles initially, calculate the number of particles remaining after $2$ years. Round your answer to the nearest integer."] [17592186087955 :question/worked-solution "The solution of $N^{\\prime}=-3N$ is $N(t)=Ce^{-3t}$. Since $N(0)=8500$, we obtain $N(2)=8500e^{-6}\\approx21.069$. There are $21$ particles remaining to the nearest integer."] [17592186087496 :question/problem "Suppose that the population $P(t)$ of a town after $t$ years can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}P} {\\textrm{d}t} = 0.02P$.\n\nIf there are currently $500$ people in the town, what will be the town's population in $3$ years?"] [17592186087496 :question/worked-solution "We note that this is an exponential growth differential equation with $r = 0.02$. Therefore, the solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nP(t) = P_0\\,e^{0.02t}\n$$\n\nwhere $P_0$ is the initial value.\n\nWe're told that the population is currently $500$, so we have $P_0 = 500$.\n\nTherefore, the population of the town at time $t$ is\n\n$$\nP(t) = 500\\,e^{0.02t}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the population after $3$ years, we substitute $t=3$ into the above, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(3) &= 500e^{0.02 \\cdot 3} \\\\\n&= 500e^{0.06} \\\\\n&\\approx 531\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the population will be approximately $531$ after $3$ years."] [17592186087562 :question/problem "Suppose that the value $V(t)$ of a particular car can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$\\dfrac{\\textrm d V}{\\textrm d t} = -0.1V$,\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in years, since the car was first purchased. If the car was purchased for $10000$ dollars, what will it be worth after $3$ years?"] [17592186087562 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the right-hand side of the equation is *negative*, which means that the car's value is *decreasing.*\n\nThis is an exponential decay differential equation with $r = 0.1$. Therefore, the general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nV(t) = V_0 e^{-0.1 t}\n$$\n\nWe know that the car was first purchased for $10000$ dollars. Therefore,\n\n$$\nV_0 = 10\\,000\n$$\n\nand we have the particular solution\n\n$$\nV(t) = 10\\,000 e^{-0.1 t}\n$$\n\nTo find the car's value after $3$ years, we substitute $t=3$ into the particular solution above. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV(3) &= 10000e^{-0.1 \\cdot 3} \\\\\n&= 10000e^{-0.3} \\\\\n&\\approx 7408\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the car will be worth approximately $7408$ dollars after three years."] [17592186087561 :question/worked-solution "The rate $N'(t)$ at which the number of followers increases is directly proportional to the number of followers $N$. Therefore, we can model the number of followers using the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d} N}{\\mathrm{d} t} = rN\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is measured in months, and $r > 0$ is the growth rate.\n\nThe solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nN(t) = N_0\\,e^{rt}\n$$\n\nwhere $N_0$ is the initial number of followers.\n\nWe're told that there are currently $600\\,000$ followers, so we have\n\n$$\nN_0 = 600\\,000\n$$\n\nTherefore, the number of followers $N$ after $t$ months is\n\n$$\nN(t) = 600\\,000 \\, e^{rt}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the growth rate, we substitute\n\n$$\nN(3)=780\\,000\n$$\n\ninto the equation above and solve for $r$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nN(3) &= 600000e^{r \\cdot 3} \\\\\n780000 &= 600000e^{3r} \\\\\ne^{3r} &= \\frac{780000}{600000} \\\\\ne^{3r} &= 1.3 \\\\\n\\ln (e^{3r}) &= \\ln (1.3) \\\\\n3r &= \\ln (1.3) \\\\\nr &= \\frac{1}{3}\\ln (1.3) \\\\\n&\\approx 0.087455\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nr\\approx 9\\%\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest percent."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186084742 17592186084743 17592186084744 17592186084745 17592186084746 17592186087079 17592186087080 17592186087081 17592186087082 17592186087083] "immutable_fields" [[17592186084741 :answer-field/correct] [17592186084741 :answer-field/id] [17592186084741 :answer-field/key] [17592186084741 :answer-field/type] [17592186087078 :answer-field/correct] [17592186087078 :answer-field/id] [17592186087078 :answer-field/key] [17592186087078 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
